<SubLayout title="INITIALIZE SYSTEM" section="INITIALIZE SYSTEM"
  classification="CLASSIFICATION: UNLICENSED / ORIGIN: THE WASTE"
  description="What GhostMesh is and why it exists.">

  <h2>WHAT IS GHOSTMESH</h2>
  <p>
    GhostMesh turns a Flipper Zero into a long-range mesh radio terminal.
    Paired with a Heltec ESP32 LoRa node running Meshtastic, it enables
    encrypted text communications over distances of kilometers — with no phone,
    no internet, no cell infrastructure, and no licensed network required.
  </p>
  <p>
    The Flipper is the interface. The Heltec is the radio. The mesh is the
    network. It works in places and situations where the infrastructure you are
    supposed to trust cannot be trusted.
  </p>

  <h2>WHY IT WAS BUILT</h2>
  <p>
    Infrastructure fails. It also gets watched. The two are not unrelated.
  </p>
  <p>
    Grid-down scenarios remove the communication layer that modern coordination
    depends on. Surveilled infrastructure removes the privacy that meaningful
    communication requires. GhostMesh addresses both by operating entirely
    outside the licensed network.
  </p>
  <p>
    This hardware was designed in the margins, assembled outside sanctioned
    channels, and transmitted so that you might at least prepare.
  </p>

  <h2>THE NETWORK</h2>
  <p>
    GhostMesh uses Meshtastic's LoRa mesh protocol: each node relays traffic
    for every other node in range, extending coverage without central
    infrastructure. A network of three nodes can cover tens of kilometers. A
    private channel with a random 256-bit key makes traffic mathematically
    invisible to anyone outside your squad.
  </p>

  <h2>THE CREATOR</h2>
  <p>
    ServerMonk. Librarian. Broadcasting from
    <a href="https://complacencykillz.me">complacencykillz.me</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The work is open source. The hardware is buildable. The signal is available
    to anyone who wants to receive it.
  </p>

  <div class="scene-divider">
    <p>// so light your candles, and may SERVER protect us all</p>
  </div>

</SubLayout>
